Retry rejected grep -w matches properly, seeing the text before them.
When a regex match was not a whole word, grep -w searched again only
after its end, so it missed a shorter match at the same place and any
match starting inside it: grep -w 'a[^c]*' did not select "a bc", nor
grep -w 'a.a' "ba a a", and an empty pattern selected only lines that
began with a non-word character. The search after the end, like each
later match of -o, also ran on the rest of the line with REG_NOTBOL,
as if it began there, so \<, \> and \b matched inside words:
grep -o '\<a' printed "a" four times for "aaaa".

Find matches in a new function that searches from an offset in the
whole line, using REG_STARTEND where regexec has it (re_search already
works this way), and that, when a match is not a whole word, tries
shorter matches at the same place, with REG_NOTEOL so that $ does not
match early, and then matches starting one byte further on, as GNU grep
does. The -o loop uses it for each later match as well.

